 6.77, "baseline_margin": 0.56, "analyst_adjustment": -0.06, "key_factors": ["Ten raters split between tossup and a slight Republican lean, with one calling it a Democratic flip", "Strong Democratic national environment offsets Huizenga's incumbency in an R-leaning district", "Latest poll (Democratic-sponsored, mid-September) shows McCann up one; earlier polling favored Huizenga", "Huizenga holds a cash edge, but outside groups have spent heavily against him while little backs McCann"], "rationale": "Race raters are split between tossup and a slight Republican edge, and prediction markets put McCann just under even, while a strong Democratic national environment and a September poll showing McCann narrowly ahead offset Huizenga's incumbency and fundraising advantages.", "watch": "Whether the national environment holds for Democrats or Huizenga's spending advantage and late outside spending move undecided voters.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Bill Huizenga (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Southwest Michigan's 4th District leans Republican by about three points, and Bill Huizenga is seeking reelection there against state Senator Sean McCann, with a Green nominee also on the ballot. Race raters largely call this a tossup: five of ten do, while Cook Political Report, Decision Desk HQ, and Inside Elections give Huizenga a small edge, and one outlet rates it a Democratic flip.\n\nThe only recent poll, a September survey from a Democratic-aligned firm, showed McCann ahead 47 to 46; earlier polls in the cycle favored Huizenga. Huizenga has raised more money and holds a cash advantage, but outside groups have spent over $1.1 million attacking him, versus little on McCann's behalf. Prediction markets price the contest just shy of even. A favorable national environment for Democrats keeps the incumbent's seat genuinely in play, with the margin expected to be razor thin.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.5309, "margin": 0.42}, {"date": "2026-09-23